{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## DECISION RECORDED — Option B approved (operator)\n\nThe operator approved **Option B**: split the overloaded `coverage.llm_unavailable` boolean rather than widen it into a vague `degraded`. Operator rationale, verbatim intent: *\"'degraded' is exactly the kind of overly broad and easily misinterpreted category that causes LLMs problems interpreting the output.\"*\n\n**This ticket is now READY-TO-FIX (no longer an open escalation).** Concrete plan:\n1. In `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py`, stop folding `LLMInputRejectedError` into the `llm_unavailable=True` arm. Keep `coverage.llm_unavailable=True` **only** for genuine availability faults (`LLMUnavailableError`/`LLMConfigError`).\n2. Add a distinct, accurately-named signal for input rejection — an `input_rejected` boolean (or equivalent) on the degraded verdict — set when the cause is `LLMInputRejectedError` (oversized/malformed prompt, content-policy refusal).\n3. Update the three consumers that branch on the boolean to read the correct signal: `plan_review/drift_floor.py:202`, `plan_review/orchestrator.py:468`, `review_bot/adapter.py:114`.\n4. Do NOT introduce a broad `degraded` category (explicitly rejected).\n\nCoordinate with f75f's `error_code_for` branch-6 narrowing (same unified principle: reserve `llm_unavailable` for real outages, route non-availability faults to accurately-named signals). Retag escalate → ready-to-fix."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788059880887247753,"uuid":"3f5b2b0b-d9ea-4804-b79d-02ee4ae4c886"}